[Music]  hello it is just after 10 gmt let's  begin with the worsening situation in  afghanistan the united nations human  rights chief has accused the taliban of  serious violations michelle bachelet  says there are credible reports of  summary executions of civilians bachelet  has urged the armed group to commit to  the rights of women and ethnic and  religious minorities she's also called  on the un human rights council to  closely monitor the taliban's actions  we have also received credible reports  of serious violation of international  humanitarian law and human rights abuses  taking place in many areas under  effective taliban control  they include among others  summary executions of civilians and all  the combat members of the afghan  national security forces  restrictions on the right of women  including the right to move around  freely and girls right to attend school  afghanistan's diverse ethnic and  religious minorities are also at risk of  violence and repression  are the leaders of the g7 nations are  scheduled to hold a virtual meeting to  discuss the crisis later on tuesday  under pressure from allies u.s president  joe biden expected to decide on whether  to keep american troops in kabul beyond  the august 31st deadline but the taliban  has warned against any extension of that  date  on the ground more people are continuing  to show up at kabul airport hoping to  get out  well charles stratford is in kabul with  the latest on the evacuation efforts  there nadine baba is in london to tell  us about that uh critical g7 meeting uh  but first let's go live to natasha  butler who is in uh paris for us so uh  natasha we heard some very strong words  at that  meeting in geneva of the unhcr didn't we  yes we heard  from the high commissioner for human  rights at this special session of the  un's human rights council  in geneva an emergency session called to  really focus on the human rights  situation in afghanistan as i said  opening speech by michelle  bachelet and she painted a very grim  picture indeed of the human rights  situation in afghanistan she said that  her office has been inundated in the  past days and weeks  with reports of human rights  abuses she says that outside for example  of the capital kabul in some of the  remote areas girls are being told that  they can't go to school women are being  forced to stay at home she talked about  human rights defenders scared for their  lives trying to flee so an incredibly  grim picture was painted by michelle  bachelet who said that the human rights  council would call on the taliban to  adhere to international rules  international  law to uphold the rights of people in  afghanistan women girls minorities  activists people who've worked in the  past for the media  or for the former government she also  said that all violence must stop on all  sides we also heard during this uh  council session that began a few hours  ago in geneva from uh afghan delegates  the former afghan ambassador to the un  giving a very impassioned speech talking  about the situations there's urging the  international community to act not to  turn away to actually  put into place real actions on the  ground not just words because the  situation is so serious not just now but  will continue to be so in the future  a humanitarian crisis is unfolding as we  speak  the people of afghanistan in particular  the thousands of human rights defenders  journalists academics professionals  civil society members and former  security personnel  who were the backbone  and we hope still will be of a  contemporary and democratic society and  whose lives and livelihoods are at risk  they deserve your full support here at  this council  at least your moral support  to be frank with you you are failing  them  but now today not next month not next  march you have the chance to redeem  yourselves  please take it  please ensure the session has a credible  and strong outcome  please do not leave this tomorrow or the  next month  money i speak to you in afghanistan  already fair that may not they may not  have a tomorrow  in our worst moments we call on you to  do better  well delegates at the human rights  council are expected to adopt a  resolution at the end  of their meeting but what  some of the delegates particularly those  two afghan speakers that you heard there  have been pushing for is a fact-finding  mission a u.n fact-finding mission which  would mean uh that u.n workers would be  deployed in afghanistan to document any  abuses and hold those responsible uh  accountable it is something that  international organizations including  human rights watch have been calling for  over the past few days we'll have to  wait and see whether or not that is part  of the final resolution  natasha thank you let's turn now to uh  the latest developments inside  afghanistan now charles stratford is  live for us in kabul and charles the  taliban government slowly taking shape  now we're hearing of some new  appointments  well i think it's early to say um that  the taliban government is taking shape  it's very important to to to note that  this information is being put out by  reuters and they're quoting a news  agency called pagewalker a local news  agency and these appointments we  understand are acting appointments they  in no way reflect any um  at this stage potential future  government  but according to the patchwork news  agency quoted by reuters the taliban  have  uh appointed a new acting finance  minister a man called gul agar  he is a what we understand was a famous  military commander in helmand province  it's also being reported that the  taliban have appointed an acting  interior minister a man called sadhara  ibrahim again a famous commander who  we've been told was very close to the  former taliban leader  and  the acting intelligence chief apparently  has also been appointed  a man called najibullah who at this  stage we are making calls to confirm  whether  these appointments have have indeed been  made and to get more information  certainly about that  what's being reported as the acting  intelligence chief  it's obviously important to recognize  that the taliban have been in  consultations with the former president  here mohammed karzai and the former  chief negotiator on behalf of ashraf  ghani abdullah abdullah in recent days  to try and flesh out the beginnings of  negotiations for a formal government a  formal administration we understand that  those negotiations are ongoing  but when you speak to analysts here the  fundamental concerns that they have  along with human rights abuses and rule  of law are also financial ones and  it's interesting that an acting finance  minister has reportedly been appointed  today because there are real concerns  about whether the taliban have the  experience to run this country and to  deal with the very great sort of  financial needs and challenges that it  faces  a lot of discussion charles about the uh  ongoing evacuations there's that g7  meeting coming up later today uh and and  whether they will extend the deadline of  of august 31st for the us to to get out  completely what's the latest we're  hearing about the situation in in kabul  airport right now  well the evacuations continue um the  americans yesterday saying it was the  the greatest number of people have been  evacuated in one day yesterday um  they're also saying that up to 200  planes are being are involved in this  effort and as you say we're getting  closer by the day  towards the august 31st deadline we know  there is pressure on biden we understand  there is increasing pressure by  other nato members um that kind of  pressure which we are expecting to see  at this g7 summit later the taliban as  we know have called august the 31st a  red line and they have warned of  consequences but let's  remind ourselves that this deadline for  the withdrawal of the us nato forces  from this country has already been  changed twice and the taliban are  looking for some sort of international  legitimacy here despite their rhetoric  of saying this is being a red line and  threatening consequences  it remains to be seen as to how forceful  they would be when they are trying to be  taken very seriously as they say by  members of the international community  and they are in such dire need of money  and money that remains locked up the u.s  having frozen around 9 billion in u.s  accounts and that's afghan cash  charles stratford in kabul thank you  well spain is warning it will have to  leave people behind in afghanistan while  germany's foreign minister has suggested  it may take longer to get people out  beyond the end of august it is the first  nato country to publicly say  it is in talks to keep kabul airport  open for evacuations into september  since we filmed it  we are in talks with the us and turkey  in order to keep kabul airport  operational to make civil evacuations of  these people possible we will have a  role to keep discussing that with the  taliban and doing so because they will  have to play a special role in that  well let's talk now to nadim barber who  is live in london for us so nadeem looks  like the g7 leaders are divided on that  extension of the deadline what's likely  to happen  well has in this virtual g7 meeting of  the leaders of uh  industrialized nations that's the us the  uk canada france germany italy and japan  uh a meeting under the chairmanship of  prime minister boris johnson but it's  also attended virtually by the nato  secretary general jens stoltenberg and  the united nations secretary general  antonio guterres  beyond what happens right now in terms  of evacuations there are big questions  that they're expected to talk about the  first is uh if and when to recognize the  new taliban administration in kabul  the 2020  agreement signed by the trump  administration  actually explicitly states that the  taliban is not recognized by the united  states  as a state beyond that there are  questions of whether in fact there  should be renewed sanctions on the  taliban to pressure them to  commit to their human rights commitments  we've been hearing what the u.n human  rights chief has been saying about  violations reports of violations coming  in already so whether to try to pressure  them to  step up and meet those commitments and  also commitments on international  relations but in the short term yes  there is pressure on president biden the  uk would like to keep its 1 000 or so  military personnel in kabul keep the  airlifts going they would like a few  more days beyond the deadline of next  tuesday but ben wallace the uk defense  minister in the last few hours has said  that is unlikely and that the uk will  not be able to do anything if the us  does withdraw on that deadline um the uh  shadow foreign secretary of the uh  opposition labour party here in the uk  is urging boris johnson not just to come  up with a plan not just to push  president biden on airlifts but for a  longer term plan including to help  countries such as pakistan which have  already received millions of afghan  refugees over the years and expected to  receive more in the near future she says  that boris johnson can play a leadership  role in trying to get the g7 nations to  try to agree some sort of assistance  plan for the  region nadeem baba thank you  the taliban insists it will allow  civilians to leave afghanistan even  after foreign forces withdraw but many  afghans are skeptical and continue to  arrive at kabul airport hoping to get  out charlotte bellis reports from there  thousands of people at kabul airport  going nowhere  beyond the gate are planes that us  president biden promised would take them  away but it's shut so they jostle for  position the criteria who is most  threatened by the taliban  my mother has been inside the airport  for the last six days but the rest of  our family stuck outside because of all  the people at the gate  this is the north gate where most people  are asked to enter through but in recent  days it has been closed because of what  the us says is security threats from  isil but for most people here the  biggest threat is a stampede is  dehydration and gunfire  more than 20 people have been killed in  the last week in and around the airport  i'm living here with my children right  now i'm scared of the crowd and all the  gunfire there's no information the us  shouldn't have announced anything if  they weren't able to take people out  on the road to the north gate this is  where the taliban presence ends and  afghan special forces begin specifically  nds-0 units the two are separated by a  piece of string  the zero units ban filming so we record  on our phones all other afghan security  forces surrendered but they did not  zero units are funded by the cia and  accused of night raids and human rights  abuses  there is a lot of danger here the nds  zero units they're doing a lot of firing  they threaten people a lot of people are  wounded  the u.s put them in charge of this  stretch of road to separate western  forces from the taliban still as we  filmed the taliban drove past unhindered  at the airport gunfire has been the main  cause of injuries these were the zero  units last week shooting appears to be  their preferred method of crowd control  the taliban has also moved in some of  its most feared fighters the badri 313  it is a special forces wing associated  with the powerful haqqani network they  are now securing the perimeter of the  airport  people are trying to reach evacuation  flights because they say they are scared  of the taliban yet it's the taliban they  must get through first followed by zero  units in a lawless stretch of land the  u.s says it is flying out tens of  thousands of people for their protection  but first making them cross a dangerous  road to safety charlotte bellas al  jazeera

